\title{Self-Improving Proofs:\\ A Refinement Calculus on Proof Complexity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-09}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous calculus of \emph{proof refinement}, making precise the idea
that a proof of a theorem can be progressively simplified and that this
simplification is a well-behaved dynamical process. Each proof $P$ of a
proposition $T$ is assigned a \textbf{complexity}
$C(P) = \mathrm{length}(P) + \mathrm{depth}(P) + \#\mathrm{lemmas}(P) \in \N$,
and a proof $P'$ \emph{refines} $P$ when it proves the same theorem strictly more
simply, $C(P') < C(P)$. We prove that refinement is a well-founded strict order,
that every nonempty family of proofs of $T$ contains a complexity-minimal member,
and consequently that as soon as $T$ is provable it possesses a globally simplest
proof $P_\infty$ --- the limit of the refinement process, which always exists. We
show the minimal complexity $C(P_\infty)$ is a well-defined invariant of $T$; that
no infinite strictly-descending refinement chain exists; that every
non-increasing refinement sequence is eventually constant (termination); and yet
that refinement chains can be arbitrarily long. The theory is instantiated on the
irrationality of $\sqrt{2}$, exhibiting an explicit chain
$C = 7 \rightsquigarrow 4 \rightsquigarrow 2$ whose minimal member is identified.
We close by isolating exactly which parts of the informal ``self-improving
proofs'' program are established and which remain open.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A proof is customarily regarded as a static artifact: once verified, it is
complete and immutable. Yet mathematical practice tells a different story. The
same theorem is reproved again and again, each generation trimming hypotheses,
merging cases, replacing bespoke arguments by reusable lemmas, until a folklore
``book proof'' emerges. This suggests treating a proof not as a fixed object but
as a \emph{state} in a dynamical system, with a transition rule ---
\emph{simplification} --- driving it toward an optimum.

This paper makes that picture precise and proves its structural backbone. We
introduce a minimal but faithful model in which the only feature of a proof we
track is a scalar complexity, and we show that the entire qualitative behavior of
proof simplification is governed by a single classical fact: the
\textbf{well-ordering of the natural numbers}. From it we extract, in a tight
dependency chain, the existence of simplest proofs, the well-definedness of a
theorem's minimal complexity, the impossibility of infinite refinement, the
termination of every improving sequence, and --- as a counterweight --- the
unboundedness of chain length.

The central conceptual payoff is a clean separation of two notions that intuition
tends to conflate: \emph{how simple can a proof of $T$ get} (a well-defined
number, the minimal complexity) versus \emph{how long simplification might take
to get there} (unbounded).

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A precise model of proofs-as-complexity-bundles and of refinement (\S2).
  \item Well-foundedness of refinement and the strict-order laws (\S3).
  \item Existence of complexity-minimal proofs and of a globally simplest proof
        $P_\infty$ whenever $T$ is provable (\S4).
  \item Well-definedness of the minimal complexity $C(P_\infty)$ (\S5).
  \item Non-existence of infinite refinement chains and termination of every
        non-increasing refinement sequence (\S6).
  \item Unboundedness of refinement chain length (\S7).
  \item A worked instantiation on the irrationality of $\sqrt2$ (\S8).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The model}

\subsection{Proofs as complexity bundles}
Fix a proposition $T$.

\begin{definition}[Proof]
A \emph{proof} of $T$ is a pair $P = (C(P),\ \mathrm{cert}(P))$, where $C(P)\in\N$
is its \textbf{complexity} and $\mathrm{cert}(P)$ is a certificate that $T$ holds.
We write $\Proof(T)$ for the type of all proofs of $T$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[The complexity as a composite]
The measure is
$C(P) = \mathrm{length}(P) + \mathrm{depth}(P) + \#\mathrm{lemmas}(P)$,
where each summand is a nonnegative integer, so $C(P)\in\N$. Modelling $C(P)$ as
an abstract element of $\N$ loses no structure: every claim about the
\emph{dynamics} of refinement is a claim about the order structure of $\N$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[The certificate]
Requiring $\mathrm{cert}(P):T$ makes $\Proof(T)$ inhabited if and only if $T$ is
true, so refinement is always a relation between genuine proofs of the same
theorem.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Refinement}
\begin{definition}[Refinement]
For $P,Q\in\Proof(T)$, say $P$ \textbf{refines} $Q$, written $P\prec Q$, iff
$C(P) < C(Q)$.
\end{definition}

Thus $P\prec Q$ means ``$P$ proves the same theorem, strictly more simply.''
Refinement is the pullback of $<$ on $\N$ along the complexity map
$C:\Proof(T)\to\N$.

\section{Refinement is a well-founded strict order}

\begin{theorem}[Well-foundedness --- the engine]\label{thm:wf}
The relation $\prec$ on $\Proof(T)$ is well-founded: there is no infinite
sequence $P_0,P_1,\dots$ with $P_{n+1}\prec P_n$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\prec$ is the pullback of $<$ on $\N$ along $C$. The inverse image of a
well-founded relation along any function is well-founded, and $<$ on $\N$ is
well-founded. Hence $\prec$ is well-founded.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transitivity]
If $P\prec Q$ and $Q\prec R$ then $P\prec R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$C(P) < C(Q) < C(R)$, and $<$ on $\N$ is transitive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Irreflexivity]
For every $P$, $\lnot(P\prec P)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$C(P) < C(P)$ is false.
\end{proof}

\section{Existence of the simplest proof and the limit $P_\infty$}

\begin{theorem}[Minimal member of a family]\label{thm:min}
Let $S\subseteq\Proof(T)$ be nonempty. Then there exists $P\in S$ such that no
$Q\in S$ refines $P$:
$\exists P\in S,\ \forall Q\in S,\ \lnot(Q\prec P)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct from Theorem~\ref{thm:wf}: a well-founded relation has a minimal element
on every nonempty subset. Concretely, $\{C(Q):Q\in S\}$ is a nonempty set of
naturals with a least element $m$; any $P\in S$ with $C(P)=m$ is minimal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The limit always exists]\label{thm:limit}
If $T$ has at least one proof $P_0$, then it has a globally simplest proof $P$:
$\exists P\in\Proof(T),\ \forall Q\in\Proof(T),\ \lnot(Q\prec P)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:min} to $S=\Proof(T)$, nonempty since $P_0\in S$.
\end{proof}

We call such a $P$ the \textbf{limit} $P_\infty$ of the refinement process.

\section{The minimal complexity is an invariant}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the minimal complexity]\label{thm:unique}
If $P$ and $Q$ are both globally simplest proofs of $T$, then $C(P)=C(Q)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $Q$ does not refine $P$, $C(P)\le C(Q)$; since $P$ does not refine $Q$,
$C(Q)\le C(P)$. By antisymmetry, $C(P)=C(Q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Minimal complexity]
For provable $T$, set $\cmin(T):=C(P_\infty)$, well-defined by
Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}.
\end{definition}

\section{No infinite refinement, and termination}

\begin{theorem}[No infinite refinement]
There is no $f:\N\to\Proof(T)$ with $f(n+1)\prec f(n)$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise the range of $f$ has, by Theorem~\ref{thm:min}, a minimal member
$f(k)$; but $f(k+1)\prec f(k)$ lies in the range, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Termination]\label{thm:term}
Let $f:\N\to\Proof(T)$ have non-increasing complexity
($n\le m \Rightarrow C(f(m))\le C(f(n))$). Then there is $N$ with
$\forall n\ge N,\ C(f(n))=C(f(N))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:min} the range has a complexity-minimal member $f(N)$. For
$n\ge N$, monotonicity gives $C(f(n))\le C(f(N))$ and minimality gives
$C(f(N))\le C(f(n))$; hence equality.
\end{proof}

\section{Chains can be arbitrarily long}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded chain length]\label{thm:long}
If $T$ holds, then for every $N\in\N$ there is a strictly descending refinement
chain of length $N+1$: a family $f:\{0,\dots,N\}\to\Proof(T)$ with $C(f(i))$
strictly decreasing in $i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For each $i\in\{0,\dots,N\}$ form $f(i)$ with the given certificate and
$C(f(i))=N-i$. Then $i<j\le N$ gives $C(f(j))=N-j<N-i=C(f(i))$; the chain has
complexities $N,N-1,\dots,0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Although every refinement process terminates (Theorem~\ref{thm:term}), there is
no uniform bound on how many strict steps it may take.
\end{corollary}

\section{Worked example: irrationality of $\sqrt{2}$}

Instantiate at $T=\mathrm{Irrational}(\sqrt2)$, a true proposition, and compare
three strategies.

\paragraph{Strategy A --- classical contradiction ($C=7$).}
Assume $\sqrt2=a/b$ in lowest terms; then $a^2=2b^2$, so $2\mid a$; write $a=2c$,
get $b^2=2c^2$, so $2\mid b$, contradicting $\gcd(a,b)=1$. Total complexity $7$.

\paragraph{Strategy B --- via prime divisibility ($C=4$).}
Use ``$p\mid n^2 \Rightarrow p\mid n$'' for $p=2$, absorbing both parity
deductions into one reusable principle. Total complexity $4$.

\paragraph{Strategy C --- the packaged theorem ($C=2$).}
Invoke the finished irrationality result as a single named fact. Complexity $2$.

\begin{proposition}[The chain $7\rightsquigarrow4\rightsquigarrow2$]
$B\prec A$ and $C\prec B$ (since $4<7$ and $2<4$), and by transitivity
$C\prec A$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Simplest of the three]
$C$ is the minimal member of $S=\{A,B,C\}$: none of $A,B,C$ has complexity $<2$,
so no member refines $C$. This is exactly the guaranteed minimal member of
Theorem~\ref{thm:min}, with minimal complexity $2$ over $S$.
\end{proposition}

Writing each proof as $(\mathrm{length},\mathrm{depth},\#\mathrm{lemmas})$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
Strategy & length & depth & \#lemmas & $C$ \\
\hline
A: classical contradiction & 4 & 2 & 1 & 7 \\
B: via prime divisibility & 2 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\
C: packaged theorem & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Each row certifies a true proof of the same theorem; the strictly decreasing
final column is the chain $7\rightsquigarrow4\rightsquigarrow2$, and the bottom
row is the minimal member guaranteed by Theorem~\ref{thm:min}. The minimal
complexity is small; the historical refinement path that reached it was long ---
the two axes of difficulty are independent.

\section{Scope and future directions}

\paragraph{What holds.} Bundling
$\mathrm{length}+\mathrm{depth}+\#\mathrm{lemmas}$ into $C\in\N$ yields, via
Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}, a well-defined \emph{minimal complexity value}
$\cmin(T)$ --- the honest counterpart of a minimal-description length within this
measure.

\paragraph{What does not.} We do not claim uniqueness of the simplest
\emph{proof object} (many proofs may attain $\cmin(T)$), nor any connection to
the uncomputable Kolmogorov complexity $K$.

\paragraph{Why a scalar suffices.} The dynamics of refinement depend only on how
complexities compare, i.e.\ on the total preorder $C$ induces on proofs. The
scalar model captures exactly this and nothing extraneous, so the theorems
isolate the order-theoretic content of the informal program. The results transfer
verbatim to any derived $C$ landing in a well-ordered set (see future
directions), making the development a reusable core.

\paragraph{A descending-chain phenomenon.} The backbone is the descending chain
condition on $\N$: no infinite strictly decreasing sequence. This condition
underlies Noetherian structures, well-founded recursion, and termination in
rewriting. Proof simplification, once complexity is well-ordered, is another
instance; well-foundedness controls \emph{whether} descent stops, never
\emph{when} (Theorem~\ref{thm:long}).

\paragraph{Future directions.}
(1)~A syntactic complexity measure derived from a genuine inductive proof-term
datatype, re-establishing the chain for the concrete $C$. (2)~Refinement rules
modeled as complexity-non-increasing rewrites on proof terms. (3)~Confluence and
canonical forms, strengthening Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} to a unique normal-form
proof. (4)~Quantitative bounds relating maximal chain length to starting
complexity for a specific rewrite system.

\section{Conclusion}

Treating proofs as living objects and refinement as a downhill flow, we have
shown the flow is globally well-behaved: it cannot cycle, cannot run forever, and
always reaches a valley floor whose height is an invariant of the theorem ---
while being free to take arbitrarily long to get there.

\end{document}

